<!-- §01 ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-bottleneck" data-code="bottleneck">
<div class="section-no">01 — The τ Bottleneck</div>
<h2>τ is everything — and it is controlled by training data</h2>

<p>DFlash achieves 4–5× wall-clock speedup by replacing slow autoregressive drafting with a 5-layer block-diffusion model that drafts 16 tokens in a <em>single forward pass</em>. But that speedup depends entirely on one number: the average acceptance length τ — how many of those 16 draft positions the target model actually accepts before hitting a wrong token.</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">Speedup formula</div>
<span class="math-var">Speedup</span> ≈ (<span class="math-var">τ</span> + 1) / (1 + <span class="math-var">t_draft</span> / <span class="math-var">t_target</span>)<br>
<span class="math-comment">// where t_draft ≪ t_target for DFlash's 5-layer drafter</span>
</div>

<p>If τ = 8 (DFlash on Math500 with Qwen3-8B), each round produces roughly 9 accepted tokens at the cost of about 1 verification step. If τ = 4 (a poorly trained drafter on the same model), each round produces 5 — the speedup nearly halves. The entire value of the DFlash architecture rests on keeping τ high across all the query types your system will actually serve.</p>

<p>Critically, τ is not a fixed property of the architecture. The same 5-layer diffusion model, same KV injection, same block size — but different training data — can produce τ values ranging from 3.2 to 8.0. That range is larger than any architectural improvement DFlash can make. <strong>Training data is the primary lever for τ.</strong></p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">τ variance across domains — Qwen3-8B, DFlash (greedy, SGLang)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click domain to highlight</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="domain-tau-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block;"></canvas>
    <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0">Bars show τ from DFlash's SGLang evaluation (single B200 GPU, concurrency=1). The 2.4× range from chat (6.42) to math (8.01) is purely driven by how well the training distribution matches each benchmark's token pattern. No architecture changes — only training data.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>This post studies four dimensions of training data that each independently move τ: what domains you include, whose responses you use, how you present training examples (anchor strategy), and how much data you train on.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-domain" data-code="domain">
<div class="section-no">02 — Domain Coverage</div>
<h2>Domain mismatch: quantified</h2>

<p>DFlash trains on approximately 800K samples from NVIDIA Nemotron Post-Training Dataset V2 combined with CodeAlpaca — deliberately spanning math, code, and general chat. The reasoning is direct: if you only train on chat data, the drafter learns chat-domain token sequences well but underperforms on math and code benchmarks where the target model uses very different vocabulary and structural patterns.</p>

<p>This is not an abstract concern. In experiments with EAGLE-style drafters (which use the same underlying principle — learn the target model's output distribution from training data), the gap between a chat-heavy and a balanced training corpus is directly measurable:</p>

<div class="results-bars" id="domain-bars">
  <div class="bar-section-head">EAGLE-3 drafter — LLaMA-3.1-8B, Math500 acceptance length</div>
  <div class="bar-row">
    <div class="bar-label">Chat-heavy (ShareGPT only)</div>
    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="40.4" style="background:rgba(107,114,128,.5)"><span>τ = 2.82</span></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="bar-row">
    <div class="bar-label">Balanced (chat+math+code)</div>
