<p>The top two bars are directly comparable EAGLE-3 experiments on the same base model with the same architecture: the only difference is whether training data includes math-domain samples. The result is a 23% increase in acceptance length (+0.66 tokens per round) purely from balanced domain coverage — no other changes.</p>

<p>The bottom four bars reveal a second pattern: even with balanced training, different domains achieve different τ values with the same DFlash model. Math problems produce the highest acceptance length (8.01) because mathematical reasoning follows highly predictable token patterns — LaTeX formulas, step-by-step arithmetic, consistent notation. Code is next (6.6–7.2); natural language chat is lowest (6.4). This is intrinsic to the domains, but domain-specific fine-tuning (for deployment-specific query mixes) can narrow the gap.</p>

