<h3>The domain-specific τ signal</h3>
<p>The per-domain τ difference in the bar chart above is itself informative. When you train a new DFlash drafter, you can diagnose whether your training mix is balanced by checking τ across benchmark categories: if Math500 τ and HumanEval τ are both above 6, the mix is working. If one is significantly below, you need more training data from that domain.</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-alignment" data-code="alignment">
<div class="section-no">03 — Target Alignment</div>
<h2>Whose responses does the drafter learn from?</h2>

<p>Even with perfectly balanced domain coverage, there is a second crucial choice: <em>whose responses</em> populate the training set. The original dataset responses are written by humans, or generated by a different model entirely (e.g., GPT-4 was used to generate many SFT datasets). But DFlash's drafter will be paired at inference time with a specific target model — say, Qwen3-8B. The drafter's job is to predict <em>that model's next tokens</em>.</p>

<p>If you train on human-written answers or answers from a different model, the drafter learns the wrong distribution. It will predict tokens that look statistically likely according to the training author, not according to Qwen3-8B. At verification time, Qwen3-8B will reject those tokens more often, lowering τ.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Distribution mismatch — dataset response vs. target model response</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to toggle view</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="btn-group">
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" id="align-btn-mismatch">Dataset responses</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="align-btn-match">Target model responses</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="align-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block;"></canvas>
    <p id="align-caption" style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0">
      <strong>Dataset responses:</strong> The drafter learns word patterns from the dataset's authors. These may differ systematically from the target model's preferred phrasing, token ordering, and vocabulary choices. Every mismatched token is a rejection at verification time.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>DFlash's training procedure directly addresses this: <em>"Instead of directly using the original dataset, we construct our training set with the responses generated by the target model for better target alignment."</em> The frozen target model (Qwen3-8B, etc.) is run on every prompt at temperature 0 (greedy), and its outputs replace the original dataset responses.</p>

<p>This has a measurable effect. SpecForge's SpecBundle experiment (using the same principle for EAGLE-3 drafters) quantified the impact directly: regenerating training responses from the target model at temperature 0.8 adds <strong>+5.3% throughput on average</strong> across benchmarks, compared to using the dataset's provided responses. On Math500 and LiveCodeBench — where the target model's style diverges most from generic dataset responses — the gap is even larger.</p>

<div class="callout purple">
  <strong>Why temperature matters:</strong> DFlash uses greedy (temperature 0) responses for its training set. SpecForge found that temperature 0.8 works better for EAGLE-3 because the drafter needs to handle non-greedy decoding at inference. For DFlash, which is primarily evaluated at temperature 0, greedy target responses are the exact distribution the drafter needs to predict.
</div>

<table class="results-table">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Training responses from</th><th>Math500 τ gain</th><th>Avg. throughput</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Dataset-provided (human / other model)</td><td class="mn">baseline</td><td class="mn">—</td></tr>
    <tr class="highlight-row"><td>Target model (temp=0.8, EAGLE-3 setting)</td><td class="mn best">+0.66 τ (+23%)</td><td class="mn best">+5.3%</td></tr>
    <tr class="highlight-row"><td>Target model (temp=0, DFlash setting)</td><td class="mn best">designed for greedy eval</td><td class="mn best">primary config</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>The hidden cost of misaligned training</h3>
<p>The alignment effect compounds with domain coverage. If you use misaligned responses <em>and</em> a poorly balanced domain mix, the combined τ penalty can be severe enough to eliminate most of DFlash's speedup advantage over EAGLE-3. Conversely, getting both right — balanced domains with target-model-generated responses — is how DFlash achieves τ = 8.01 on Math500, which translates to the 5.1× end-to-end speedup reported in the paper.</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-anchor" data-code="anchor">
<div class="section-no">04 — Anchor Sampling</div>
<h2>How you present training data matters as much as what data you use</h2>

<p>Beyond <em>what</em> data you collect, <em>how</em> you turn that data into training examples determines coverage. DFlash's drafter predicts a block of 16 masked tokens starting from a clean anchor token. The question is: where in the response do you place those anchor positions?</p>

<p>The naive approach — "standard block construction" — divides each response into non-overlapping fixed blocks. A 512-token response becomes 32 fixed blocks of 16 tokens each. Every training run sees the same anchor positions. This is efficient but has two problems: the drafter only learns to condition on a fixed set of context feature positions, and it is never exposed to the full variety of hidden states the target model produces at different sequence positions.</p>

<p>DFlash's solution: <strong>random anchor sampling</strong>. For each sequence at each training epoch, 512 anchor positions are <em>randomly</em> sampled from the response. Each anchor position produces a fresh training example — a different context feature, a different block of tokens to predict. Over 6 epochs, the drafter sees the same data but from hundreds of different vantage points.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Standard blocks vs. random anchor sampling — coverage comparison</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to animate</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="btn-group">
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" id="anc-btn-standard">Standard blocks</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="anc-btn-random">Random anchors</button>
    </div>
