    <p id="anchor-caption" style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0">
      <strong>Standard blocks:</strong> Each response is divided into fixed non-overlapping 16-token windows. The drafter trains on the same anchor positions every epoch — limited diversity of context features.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The quantitative effect is substantial (DFlash, 100K samples, Qwen3-4B):</p>

<table class="results-table">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Anchor strategy</th><th>Math500 τ</th><th>Math500 speedup</th><th>HumanEval τ</th><th>HumanEval speedup</th><th>MT-Bench speedup</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Standard (fixed blocks)</td><td class="mn">4.94</td><td class="mn">4.13×</td><td class="mn">3.86</td><td class="mn">3.29×</td><td class="mn">2.13×</td></tr>
    <tr class="highlight-row"><td>Random anchor sampling</td><td class="mn best">5.64</td><td class="mn best">4.69×</td><td class="mn best">4.61</td><td class="mn best">3.90×</td><td class="mn best">2.38×</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Improvement</td><td class="mn">+0.70 τ (+14%)</td><td class="mn">+0.56× (+14%)</td><td class="mn">+0.75 τ (+19%)</td><td class="mn">+0.61× (+19%)</td><td class="mn">+0.25× (+12%)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Random anchor sampling improves τ by 14–19% depending on domain, with zero change to the dataset or the model architecture. This is purely a data <em>presentation</em> effect — the same 100K samples, arranged differently, produce a substantially better drafter.</p>

<h3>Why random anchors help</h3>
<p>Two mechanisms are at work. First, <strong>diverse context features</strong>: when anchor positions are random, the drafter is trained to predict from target hidden states at arbitrary sequence positions — not just at positions that are multiples of 16. This forces generalization over the full range of context feature patterns the model produces. Second, <strong>data augmentation</strong>: a 512-token response with 512 randomly sampled anchors produces many more distinct training examples than 32 fixed blocks. Over 6 epochs, this effectively multiplies the training set size with no additional data collection cost.</p>

<div class="callout cyan">
  <strong>Connection to long-context training:</strong> EAGLE-3's training-time test approach is expensive for long sequences because it requires running the full target model for each unroll step. DFlash's random anchor sampling achieves a similar diversity benefit more efficiently: by fixing the number of anchor positions per sequence (512) and varying their locations, training cost stays bounded even as sequence length grows.
</div>
</section>

<!-- §05 ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-scale" data-code="scale">
<div class="section-no">05 — Data Scale</div>
<h2>When more data helps — and when it doesn't</h2>

<p>Adding more training data sounds like an obvious win, but speculative decoding drafter research has documented a crucial non-obviousness: <em>the benefit of scaling data depends on the architecture</em>. The original EAGLE showed essentially flat returns when data was scaled beyond ~100K samples. EAGLE-3 — and DFlash — fixed this, but for different reasons.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Data scaling law: EAGLE (plateau) vs. EAGLE-3 (linear) vs. DFlash</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover for values</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="scale-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block;"></canvas>
    <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0">
      EAGLE's feature-regression loss acts as a capacity bottleneck: the drafter is constrained to predict target <em>hidden states</em>, not token distributions, which limits how much additional data can improve performance. EAGLE-3 removes this constraint. DFlash has no feature-regression loss by design — the diffusion drafter is always trained end-to-end to predict tokens, not intermediate features.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Why EAGLE plateaued</h3>
<p>EAGLE's training objective includes a feature-prediction (MSE) loss that forces the draft model's hidden states to match the target model's hidden states. This constraint, while intuitively appealing, effectively limits the drafter's representational capacity: it is optimized to be a <em>feature mimic</em>, not a <em>token predictor</em>. With more training data, there is simply nowhere for the model to improve — the MSE constraint is already satisfied, and further data does not increase acceptance length.</p>

<p>EAGLE-3 removed the feature-regression loss entirely, allowing the drafter to optimize directly for token prediction. The result was a genuine scaling law: <em>"scaling up data provides linear improvements for EAGLE-3"</em> — speedup continues to grow with data volume, roughly proportionally to the logarithm of training set size.</p>

<h3>DFlash's natural alignment</h3>
<p>DFlash's block-diffusion drafter avoids this problem by construction. The training objective is always cross-entropy over token predictions — there is no feature-regression term. The drafter is free to allocate its representational capacity however it needs to maximize token prediction accuracy, which is exactly what acceptance length rewards. DFlash reports training on ~800K samples from Nemotron V2 + CodeAlpaca — approximately 4–8× the scale of early EAGLE experiments — and achieves the corresponding τ gains.</p>

<div class="callout purple">
  <strong>The practical implication:</strong> If you are training a DFlash drafter for a specialized application (e.g., a code-only deployment), you can scale training data within that domain without hitting a plateau. The diffusion architecture's lack of a feature-regression bottleneck means more domain-specific data translates proportionally to higher τ within that domain.
</div>

<h3>Scale vs. quality trade-off</h3>
<p>At 100K samples, DFlash already achieves strong performance (Math500 τ ≈ 5.64 with random anchors). The full 800K dataset pushes this to τ = 8.01. But that 6× scale increase does not produce a 6× τ improvement — the relationship is sub-linear. In practice, DFlash's authors report that the biggest gains come early: well-curated 100K samples already capture most of the benefit that 800K provides, which is why their ablations use 100K as the reference.</p>
</section>

<!-- §06 ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-recipe" data-code="recipe">
<div class="section-no">06 — Training Recipe</div>
<h2>A recipe for training DFlash drafters</h2>

<p>Putting the four dimensions together produces a concrete recipe. Each step has a quantified τ contribution; they are not fully additive (there are interactions), but together they span the difference between a weak drafter (τ ≈ 3.5) and DFlash's best (τ ≈ 8.0).</p>

<div class="step-list" id="recipe-steps">
  <div class="step-box active">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Balanced domain coverage — math + code + chat</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Train on a mixture covering all query domains you expect in deployment. DFlash uses Nemotron V2 (math-heavy) + CodeAlpaca (code) for English. Without this, τ on underrepresented domains falls by 0.5–1.5 tokens.</div>
      <div class="step-code">~800K samples · math ≈ 40% · code ≈ 40% · chat ≈ 20%</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Regenerate responses from the target model</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Run the frozen target model on every prompt at temperature 0 (or 0.8 for sampling settings) and use its outputs as training labels — not the dataset's original responses. This aligns the training distribution with the model the drafter will be paired with.</div>
      <div class="step-code">target.generate(prompt, temp=0, do_sample=False)  # →  training label</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Use random anchor sampling — not fixed blocks</div>
      <div class="step-desc">For each sequence at each epoch, randomly sample 512 anchor positions rather than using fixed non-overlapping blocks. This augments the effective training data volume and exposes the drafter to diverse context-feature patterns. Gain: +14–19% τ improvement with zero new data.</div>
      <div class="step-code">anchors = random.sample(range(seq_len), 512)  # per epoch</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Apply exponential loss decay by position</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Weight earlier tokens within each block more heavily: wₖ = exp(−(k−1)/γ), where γ = 7 for block-16. An error at position 1 of a 16-token block rejects all 15 subsequent tokens; an error at position 15 costs only 1. The loss schedule reflects this asymmetry.</div>
      <div class="step-code">w_k = exp(-(k-1) / gamma)  # k=1 is highest priority</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">5</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Scale training data — but quality first</div>
      <div class="step-desc">DFlash scales to ~800K samples for production models, but well-curated 100K already captures most of the gain. DFlash's diffusion architecture does not have EAGLE's feature-regression bottleneck, so data scaling translates proportionally to higher τ — but diminishing returns apply after the first 300K.</div>
      <div class="step-code">n_samples = 800_000  # Nemotron V2 + CodeAlpaca, target-regenerated</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">6</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Train 6 epochs with AdamW, lr=6×10⁻⁴, cosine decay</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Standard recipe: AdamW, lr=6e-4, gradient clip=1.0, cosine schedule, warmup ratio=0.04. 6 epochs on the full dataset (or equivalently, augmented 100K with 512 random anchors per sequence per epoch). Shared embeddings and LM head with the frozen target model.</div>
      <div class="step-code">optimizer = AdamW(lr=6e-4, clip=1.0); epochs = 6</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="step-prev" disabled>‹ Prev</button>
  <button id="step-next">Next ›</button>
  <span class="step-prog" id="step-prog">Step 1 of 6</span>
</div>

