<div class="callout cyan">
  <strong>For reasoning models:</strong> DFlash trains a separate drafter for Qwen3 models with thinking mode enabled, using the same balanced recipe but adding reasoning traces (chain-of-thought) to the training set. The result is τ = 5.17–5.82 on GPQA/MATH-500/AIME25, maintaining 4.2–4.6× speedup even for long CoT outputs.
</div>

<p>The bottom line: DFlash's training data design is not accidental. Each of the four dimensions — domain balance, target alignment, anchor strategy, and scale — contributes independently to τ. Removing any one of them leaves measurable performance on the table. Implementing all four together is what takes τ from the 3.5–4.5 range (where a generic approach lands) to the 6–8 range where DFlash's reported results live.</p>
</section>

<!-- Listen-friendly hidden summary -->
<div class="listen-friendly-content" style="display: none;" aria-hidden="true">
  <div lang="en">
    <h2>Audio Summary: What Training Data Actually Does to DFlash's Acceptance Length</h2>
    <p>DFlash is a speculative decoding framework that uses a 5-layer block-diffusion model to draft 16 tokens in a single forward pass, conditioned on the target model's hidden states. Its speedup — between 4 and 5 times over autoregressive decoding — depends critically on the acceptance length tau: how many of those 16 draft tokens the target model accepts before finding an error.</p>
    <p>Four training data dimensions determine tau. First, domain coverage: training only on chat data leaves math and code tau 20-30% lower than a balanced mix of math, code, and general text. DFlash uses 800K samples from NVIDIA Nemotron V2 and CodeAlpaca spanning all three domains. Second, target alignment: instead of using the original dataset responses, DFlash regenerates all training labels by running the frozen target model on every prompt. This alone adds 5 percent throughput compared to using dataset-provided answers. Third, anchor sampling: DFlash randomly samples 512 anchor positions per training sequence at each epoch rather than using fixed block positions. This improves tau by 14 to 19 percent across all benchmarks with zero new data. Fourth, data scale: unlike the original EAGLE which plateaued with more data, DFlash's diffusion architecture has no feature-regression bottleneck, so scaling from 100K to 800K samples continues to improve tau. The resulting system achieves tau of 8.01 on Math500 and 6.63 on HumanEval with Qwen3-8B.</p>
  </div>
  <div lang="zh">
    <h2>语音摘要：训练数据如何影响DFlash的接受长度</h2>
    <p>DFlash是一种推测解码框架，使用5层块扩散模型在单次前向传播中并行起草16个token，通过目标模型的隐层状态进行条件化。其4到5倍的加速效果，核心在于接受长度τ——目标模型在发现错误前平均接受多少个起草token。</p>
    <p>训练数据的四个维度决定了τ的高低。第一是领域覆盖：仅使用聊天数据训练时，数学和代码领域的τ比均衡混合（数学+代码+通用文本）低20到30个百分点。DFlash使用来自NVIDIA Nemotron V2和CodeAlpaca的80万样本，覆盖三个领域。第二是目标对齐：DFlash不使用数据集原始答案，而是用冻结的目标模型对每个prompt重新生成训练标签，仅此一项就可提升5%的吞吐量。第三是锚点采样：DFlash在每个训练周期对每条序列随机采样512个锚点位置，而非使用固定块位置，无需新增数据即可将τ提升14%至19%。第四是数据规模：与原始EAGLE因特征回归损失而遭遇缩放瓶颈不同，DFlash的扩散架构没有此限制，从10万到80万样本的扩展可持续提升τ。在Qwen3-8B上，最终结果为Math500的τ=8.01，HumanEval的τ=6.63。</p>
  </div>
</div>

